## parser: the shared parser reads the day as the month

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser read every date month first, so
"7 April" came out as 4 July. This is the bug the #4471 page describes. It covers reports from
the app, from email and from the booking-v2 form, at every tenant. The desk counts the `one-day`
reports here too, where the tenant rule files them. Fixed on 5 October by "fix(dates): parse
dd/mm for eu locales", the change that closed #4471.

## hint: booking-v3 tells people to type the month first

The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. At a day-first tenant, people type
what the hint says, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The date is
already flipped before the parser sees it, so the parser fix does not reach these reports.
Engineering won't fix it: the booking-v3 form is retired, so no new reports come in this way.

<ProblemReports ticket={4471} problem="hint" />

## one-day: the date moves by one day

A flipped day and month moves a date by weeks or months, never by a day. The desk read these
reports as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`). The tenant rule files them under `parser`, and the
desk counts them there, but they describe something else: the same meeting on two days in two
cities, a date shown as the day before, an invite older than its booking, one month that is
always a day off. The parser fix does not explain them, and nothing else has shipped for them.
The cause is not known yet. Time zones and the day boundary are the first thing to check, then
June.

<ProblemReports ticket={4471} problem="one-day" quote />

## Why it stayed one ticket

- Triage matches on the subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
  Most subjects in #4471 turn up under more than one problem, so the subject cannot tell them
  apart.
- Intake tags come from the reporter's browser, not from the tenant. The "tagged as" column shows
  where intake and the tenant rule disagree.
- The ticket export lists some reports twice. Count by id; every table on this page does.

